00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.500
APOORV SAXENA: Yes.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.510
In fact, at Google
across the board

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:58.340
gets more than 10,000 service
requests, customer service

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.660
touch points, on a single day.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.340
And so as a result,
we at Google,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.300
we believe great customer
service is an important part

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.039
of a great product experience.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:13.660
So we monitor our channels not
only in the traditional email

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.030
and phone calls in terms
of customer requests,

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:18.130
but we also monitor
Facebook, Twitter,

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.840
and so on and so forth.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.470
But to get a differentiated
customer service experience

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.510
is not only important to monitor
all the different channels

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.680
the customer can reach you.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.670
It's also important to make
sure the customer request

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:34.079
journey is differentiated
across the lifecycle.

00:02:34.079 --> 00:02:36.370
SARA ROBINSON: And what does
a typical customer journey

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:37.235
look like?

00:02:37.235 --> 00:02:38.860
APOORV SAXENA: So
lets take an example.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.040
Again, it varies by
company by company.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.130
But we just did a
very high level view

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.190
of how it could look like
in a situation like this.

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:51.220
So on one side, you
have customers' touch

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.990
points across different
channels, as I said.

00:02:53.990 --> 00:02:57.310
Whether it could be a phone or
an email or Twitter feed coming

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.190
in, the very first
thing you typically

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:01.750
do when a customer
request comes in

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:05.170
is try to understand
who the customer is.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:09.400
So typically you will
tap into your CRM system

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.530
to understand who
the customer is.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:14.657
The next level is, not
every customer request

00:03:14.657 --> 00:03:16.240
can be prioritized
as a high priority.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:19.900
So try to understand what the
priority of the request is.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.410
And then you route that
request to the appropriate CSR.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.420
So it's a pretty
simplified view,

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:28.060
but across these
four steps, you still

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.970
have huge opportunity
for machine

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:31.420
learning to make an impact.

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.040
Number one, on the
left of the funnel,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:39.700
you will see that most
of the customer requests

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:42.370
are well understood if
they are coming from email.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.200
Phone is completely
handled differently.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.670
How do you make sure if I can
transcribe all my audio calls

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.640
into text and then
run some analysis,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.360
there's a huge
opportunity there.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.800
Number two, typically you can
route your customer requests

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.810
without even understanding
what's in it.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.000
What if you could
use natural language

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.760
to understand and parse what's
being said in the customer

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:04.840
service request?

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:09.670
And the third, if you want to
route your customer service

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.220
requests, if you know
if it is about shipping

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:15.820
or if it is about pricing, you
can route it to different CSRs.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:19.450
Again, huge leap of
improvement in productivity

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:21.640
if you can route
it intelligently.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.400
So those are the opportunities.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.090
So at Google, we have provided
you a set of portfolio,

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.060
machine learning
portfolio, that helps you

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:33.200
tackle a lot of these problems.

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.170
On the left hand
side, we have what

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:38.050
we call pretrained
machine learning models

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:41.766
where you don't need a data
science degree to actually use

00:04:41.766 --> 00:04:42.640
some of these models.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.190
These are RESTful
APIs that you can call

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:47.590
using a single line of code.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:49.930
And what it helps you
do is understand what's

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.300
in a major audio or video.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.930
On the right hand
side of this spectrum

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:58.170
is if you want to
build a custom model

00:04:58.170 --> 00:05:00.790
for your own particular need,
if you want to do a text

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.040
classifier to route your
tickets differently,

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.460
you probably will use an open
source deep learning framework

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:09.220
like TensorFlow and run it
on the Cloud Machine Learning

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:10.120
engine.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.030
And that's the full spectrum
that gives you the opportunity

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.320
to build to your need.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.420
So to talk about each of these
key products that are useful,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:23.002
I'll hand it over to Sara
to walk you to the products.

00:05:23.002 --> 00:05:24.210
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks Apoorv.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:27.690
So I'm going to cover the Cloud
Speech and the Cloud Natural

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.580
Language API since
those are particularly

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:32.260
relevant to customer service.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.080
Let's start with the Speech API.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.030
How many of you have
used the OK Google

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:37.590
functionality on your phone?

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:38.970
Anybody here?

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.840
Looks like a little
more than half.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:43.320
So the Speech API essentially
exposes the functionality

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.720
of OK Google to
developers, and lets

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.120
you do speech to text
transcription in over 80

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.150
languages.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.180
So here's an overview of the
features of the Speech API.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.300
You can transcribe text in
over 80 languages and variants.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:59.920
And it supports both
batch and streaming mode.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.410
So you can send it a
complete audio file.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.710
You can also send it a
stream of continuous audio,

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.230
and it'll transcribe the audio
as the stream is coming in.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:11.580
It can detect inappropriate
content in your audio.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.440
And then you can, once
you've got the text,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.200
you can analyze it with other
Google Cloud Machine Learning

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.690
APIs, which I'm going to
talk about momentarily.

00:06:21.299 --> 00:06:23.090
So this is what a
request to the Speech API

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.170
would look like in Python.

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.930
So you create your
service request.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:28.596
And there's a couple
of different things

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:30.310
you need to pass to Speech API.

00:06:30.310 --> 00:06:32.540
You tell it the encoding
type of the audio--

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.790
here we're using LINEAR16--

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.076
the sample rate in hertz.

00:06:37.076 --> 00:06:38.700
And then you pass it
the language code.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.970
If you leave this parameter
out, it'll default to English.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:43.010
Otherwise if you're using a
language other than English,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.620
you just need to tell it the
language code of the language

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.510
you'd like to transcribe.

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:49.790
And then you pass it either
a Google Cloud Storage

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:54.830
URI of your audio file, or
you can give it a local file

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:56.390
to transcribe.

00:06:56.390 --> 00:06:57.640
And that's all you have to do.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.180
So the beauty of these
machine learning APIs--

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.391
I like to call them
friendly machine learning--

00:07:01.391 --> 00:07:03.890
is they give you access to these
pretrained models with just

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:05.600
a single REST API request.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.250
So you don't really
have to understand

00:07:07.250 --> 00:07:08.930
what's going on in
the underlying model.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.670
So that's the Speech API.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:15.640
So with the Speech API,
we're transcribing audio.

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.182
Once we get that
text transcription,

00:07:17.182 --> 00:07:18.640
we likely don't
want to stop there.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:20.407
There's probably
more analysis we

00:07:20.407 --> 00:07:22.990
want to do on it, especially in
the customer service industry.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:26.890
Once we get our customer's
call, maybe it's a voice mail,

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:29.390
we're going to want to do
more analysis on that text.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:32.560
And that's where the Natural
Language API comes into play.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.950
What it does is it lets you
extract entities, sentiment,

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.630
and syntax from text.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:41.260
So I want to take a deeper
dive into those three features

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.430
that it lets you do.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.460
So for the entities example,
I'm a big Harry Potter fan.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:47.350
So I take in this
sentence about JK Rowling

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.120
from her Wikipedia page.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:51.370
And let's see what the
entity extraction end

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:55.510
point will give us back if
we send it this sentence.

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:58.300
So it's able to extract
these five entities.

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:02.086
And it's going to give us
some JSON data on each one.

00:08:02.086 --> 00:08:03.817
And one thing you'll
notice here is

00:08:03.817 --> 00:08:05.650
that those first three
entities are actually

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:06.400
all the same thing.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.900
They're actually
just different ways

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.156
of saying JK Rowling's name.

00:08:09.156 --> 00:08:10.780
Robert Galbraith is
actually a pen name

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:12.790
she used for a
later book series.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.230
But I won't get into that.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:17.120
So they all point
to the same entity.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.730
So for the entity data
we get back the name,

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.180
the type of entity it
is-- she's a person--

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.430
and then some metadata on her.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.340
So the API won't
necessarily return metadata

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.310
if it's an entity that
doesn't have a Wikipedia page.

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:31.810
But in this case,
there definitely

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.360
is a Wikipedia page
for JK Rowling.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:35.530
So it will give
us the MID, which

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.260
is an ID that maps to
Google's Knowledge Graph

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:40.570
so that you think you can
then call the Knowledge Graph

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.179
API to get more
data on this entity.

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:46.240
And then we get the
Wikipedia URL for her.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.580
And the other JSON
responses looks similar.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.580
For British, it maps
it to a location.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:53.980
We also get the MID
and the Wikipedia URL.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.010
And notice that the Wikipedia
URL points to the United

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:58.210
Kingdom Wikipedia page.

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:01.999
So if instead the text
said UK or United Kingdom,

00:09:01.999 --> 00:09:03.665
it would still point
to the same entity.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.690
And then for Harry
Potter it look similar.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:10.420
We get the entity
type as location.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:12.080
That should actually be person.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:13.120
It's a mistake.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:15.910
And then it points to Harry
Potter Wikipedia page.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:20.610
The second thing that
the Natural Language

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.190
API lets you do is analyze
the sentiment of something.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.500
So something this is useful
for is customer feedback.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.208
In this case this is
something that might

00:09:27.208 --> 00:09:28.530
be on a restaurant review.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.450
The food is excellent.

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:31.230
I would definitely go back.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:34.560
So it returns two values for
us to understand the sentiment.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:36.630
The first is magnitude.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.300
And this tells us the overall
strength of the sentiment,

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:41.730
regardless of whether
it's positive or negative.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.970
And this will change based
on the length of the text.

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.624
It can range from 0 to infinity.

00:09:46.624 --> 00:09:48.540
This text is pretty
short, which is why we get

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.220
a pretty small number there.

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.800
The score value is going
to tell us whether it's

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:53.770
positive or negative.

00:09:53.770 --> 00:09:56.197
So this ranges from
negative 1 to 1.

00:09:56.197 --> 00:09:58.530
And we can see that this is
a pretty positive statement.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.630
So it's almost all
the way at one.

00:10:02.979 --> 00:10:05.145
And then a final feature
of the Natural Language API

00:10:05.145 --> 00:10:06.540
is analyzing syntax.

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.565
And this gets a bit more into
the linguistic details of text.

00:10:10.565 --> 00:10:11.940
And its visualization
is actually

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:15.120
pulled from the products page
for the National Language API.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.160
So anybody can go
here and try it out,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.110
create a visualization
with your own text

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.660
without having to write
any code to get started.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.730
There's a bunch of
data it returns here

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:24.940
on the syntax of your text.

00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.840
So the sentence we have is
the Natural Language API

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.760
helps us understand text.

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.310
And lets start with that
top row, those green arrows.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:35.310
That's what we call a dependency
parse tree, which basically

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:36.900
tells us how the
words in the sentence

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.420
relate to each other,
which words depend

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:42.110
on other words in the sentence.

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.400
That second row is
the parse label.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:47.370
And that's basically the role
of each word in a sentence.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.130
So we can see that
helps is the root verb.

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.640
API is the nominal subject.

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.690
The third row is
just the raw text.

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:56.820
And then that purple
row is what we

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:00.540
call the lemma, which is the
canonical form of a word.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.520
So we can see that
the word is helps,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:03.900
but the lemma is actually help.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.580
So if you want to
count the occurrences

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.410
of a particular word, you
can use the canonical form

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:11.590
so that helps and help don't
count as two different words.

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:14.160
And then in the red row we
get the part of speech--

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.960
verb, noun,
adjective, et cetera.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:20.910
And then finally we got
additional morphology details

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.790
on each token in our sentence.

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:25.990
So that's what the syntax
annotation looks like.

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:29.010
So with the Speech and
Natural Language APIs,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.480
they work in many
different cases,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.240
but there may be situations
where your system has

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.700
a lot of different custom data,
custom entities that you're

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:36.840
looking for.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:39.390
And in this case you would
want to use something

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:41.656
to build and train
your own models.

00:11:41.656 --> 00:11:43.530
And I'm going to hand
it over to Apoorv forum

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.545
to talk about Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:11:45.545 --> 00:11:46.670
APOORV SAXENA: Sounds good.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:47.720
Thank you.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:51.140
So imagine I was talking about
a customer service request

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:55.980
or customer phone call coming
in, talking really agitated

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:00.520
and saying, hey, my
shipping was not delivered.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:04.320
So again, using Speech API,
you have transcribed that audio

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.200
into text using
sentiment analysis,

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.940
using Natural Language API.

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:13.230
You have figured out this
is a very negative comment.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.570
As a result, your
system so far has

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:17.350
identified this is
a very high priority

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.930
call that you need to route.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:23.400
Next thing you need to know is
what kind of requests it is.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:24.460
Is it a shipping request?

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:25.870
Is it about pricing issue?

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:27.840
It's about support issue,
so on and so forth.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:31.740
And this is where your
Cloud Machine Learning API--

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.590
sorry, the Cloud
Machine Engine comes in.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:39.180
So to do this classification of
whether a particular request is

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.190
a shipping request
or a pricing request,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:44.390
you will use what we typically
call text classification.

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:46.490
Text classification
is pretty simple.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:48.230
What you do is look
at the document,

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.690
or in this case email or any
audio converted into text,

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.420
and classify it into
a label, a predefined

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:57.940
label that you have
relevant to your use case.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.360
So a classic example,
sentiment analysis

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.910
essentially is an example
of text classification.

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:06.255
What you're doing is
looking at a document

00:13:06.255 --> 00:13:08.550
and classifying that this
is a positive document

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.004
or a negative document
in terms of polarity.

00:13:11.004 --> 00:13:12.670
Same thing with
document classification.

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:15.620
So you can not only just do a
particular document classified

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:19.740
as one label, you can say
this ticket is about shipping

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:22.470
and pricing, because
a customer can

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:27.210
talk about more than one
subject in a particular email.

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.970
So that's the example
of text classification.

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:32.490
And building a text
classifier traditionally

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.100
required a pretty
detailed understanding

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:38.910
of the underlying algorithms
and the infrastructure.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:41.190
With TensorFlow and Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:13:41.190 --> 00:13:44.580
all this is simplified
to a significant degree.

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.825
A typical text classification
on a customer service

00:13:47.825 --> 00:13:48.700
would look like this.

00:13:48.700 --> 00:13:51.200
There are two steps involved
in any building any classified.

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.950
One is the training step.

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:54.840
Another is the serving step.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:57.900
In the training step, what
you're doing is you're taking

00:13:57.900 --> 00:14:01.520
a set of labelled documents--
in this case it will be service

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.770
requests labeled as shipping
or pricing issues or customer

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.860
service issues and
so on and so forth.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:10.717
You feed that and
create a classifier.

00:14:10.717 --> 00:14:12.175
And the way you
create a classifier

00:14:12.175 --> 00:14:14.605
is basically extract
out key features

00:14:14.605 --> 00:14:16.230
that you're creating
the classifier on.

00:14:16.230 --> 00:14:18.110
In this case the
feature could be

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:21.825
words or bag of words or
sentence, so on and so forth.

00:14:21.825 --> 00:14:23.200
And then you feed
your classifier

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:24.709
and train your model.

00:14:24.709 --> 00:14:26.250
And once you have
trained your model,

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:28.860
if you got the results that you
expected in terms of your test

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:32.184
data, you then use
that trained model

00:14:32.184 --> 00:14:33.600
on a cloud machine
learning engine

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.580
to serve for your
production environment.

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.850
In the production environment
it's pretty simple.

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:40.530
You get a new service ticket.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:44.520
You send it to the classifier
being served on the Cloud

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.780
Machine Learning Engine.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:47.970
Out comes the label, in
this case whether it's

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:49.719
a shipping request or
is a pricing request

00:14:49.719 --> 00:14:51.750
and so on and so forth.

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:55.320
So this is-- again, we haven't
gone to typically a text

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.380
classifier using TensorFlow
because TensorFlow provides

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:01.520
a set of very
sophisticated libraries

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:03.380
that helps you do
the preprocessing

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.180
and the postprocessing
on text very easily.

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:08.330
So I'm hiding a lot
of those details here.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.070
But no more than
80 lines of code

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:12.610
you can build a code classifier.

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:14.900
And so for example in this
case, the very first step

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:17.990
is you can specify
the model you want

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:19.610
to use to build your classifier.

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:23.180
So here in this case,
I'm using a bag of words.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:27.560
Bag of words doesn't take into
account the order of the words.

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.720
So for example, for
shipping requests,

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:31.912
as long as there are words
like shipping, delivery,

00:15:31.912 --> 00:15:33.620
date of delivery, and
so on and so forth,

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:36.720
it will classify that request
as a shipping request.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:40.580
And for pricing, if there are
words of pricing, discounts,

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:43.340
and so on and so forth, that
will use it to classify--

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:47.960
the model will use that to
classify it as a pricing issue.

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:50.030
So that's the very
simplified bag of words.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:52.730
You can try out different
models as per your need

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:54.470
and the accuracy that you need.

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:56.830
And again, this TensorFlow
allows you to sort of change

00:15:56.830 --> 00:15:59.171
the model in very simple ways.

00:15:59.171 --> 00:16:00.920
And once you trained,
the first thing once

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.970
you've specified the model
to use, you create a model.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:07.880
And then once you've trained the
model, you run the prediction.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:10.550
And then typically you will
run this predictive model

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:14.030
or attained model on a Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.080
So that's it.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:16.190
Again, very simple.

00:16:16.190 --> 00:16:19.970
You went from step one, Speech
API using Language API and now

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:23.710
tax classification
to route your ticket.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:25.110
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks Apoorv.

00:16:25.110 --> 00:16:27.046
So we've heard
about a few products

00:16:27.046 --> 00:16:28.170
that can help us with this.

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.630
But to get a really good
understanding of it,

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:32.900
it would be good to see
a real customer use case.

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:34.836
So I'm going to bring
up Dan from Ocado.

00:16:34.836 --> 00:16:35.710
Everyone welcome Dan.

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:38.450
Hi Dan.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.380
Dan is the Head
of Data at Ocado.

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:43.910
And he's been in IT
for 20 plus years.

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:46.530
and always been passionate
about data driving businesses

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:47.204
forward.

00:16:47.204 --> 00:16:48.870
So Dan, could you
tell us a little bit--

00:16:48.870 --> 00:16:51.355
give us some background on
Ocado and explain the problem

00:16:51.355 --> 00:16:53.230
you're trying to solve
with machine learning.

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:55.140
DAN NELSON: Sure.

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:56.640
So this slide kind
of says it all.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.940
We're an online supermarket.

00:16:58.940 --> 00:17:02.760
We're based only in the
UK, so quite a few of you

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.849
probably won't have heard of us.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:07.319
What it doesn't
tell you is in fact

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:12.060
we're the world's largest
dedicated online grocery

00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:13.380
business.

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:16.770
We have about half a
million active customers

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:21.042
and a revenue of $1.5 billion.

00:17:21.042 --> 00:17:22.680
We've been around 17 years.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:24.700
I think that's the
time that Diane

00:17:24.700 --> 00:17:29.190
said is an average yesterday,
which was a bit funny,

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:33.060
and have basically
innovated and disrupted

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:37.200
the grocery market in the
UK since our inception.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.760
We actually have a
thousand engineers.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.730
So we're more a
technology company

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:46.560
who run retail and operation
logistics businesses

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:49.890
than a pure play retailer.

00:17:49.890 --> 00:17:53.830
And those operations are
kind of part of the model.

00:17:53.830 --> 00:17:58.380
We run what we call CFCs,
customer fulfillment centers.

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:02.540
These are large scale
automated warehouses.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:06.440
By large scale I'm talking
about four football pitches

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.780
as a footprint,
four stories high,

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:15.420
with about 20 miles worth of
conveyor belt within them.

00:18:15.420 --> 00:18:19.466
They basically take products
from suppliers on pallets,

00:18:19.466 --> 00:18:20.840
and then they
distribute them out

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:25.200
at the other end as individual
items for customer orders.

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:30.030
They process about 10
million items a week

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.760
and complete an order
every 2 and 1/2 seconds.

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:36.800
And we have two of those, what
we call our kind of legacy ones

00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:39.870
now, because as part of
disrupting the industry,

00:18:39.870 --> 00:18:41.770
we disrupt ourselves.

00:18:41.770 --> 00:18:45.180
And we've recently created
a new model of warehouse.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:48.450
It operates as a hive, a three
dimensional dense storage

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:53.010
grid with robots running over
the top of it, which basically

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:57.990
give us increased efficiency
and increased accuracy.

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:01.920
And all this technology and
all this operation efficiency

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:06.210
is all because we want
exceptional customer service.

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:10.500
The grocery market is extremely
competitive around the world,

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:13.460
but in the UK especially.

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:16.530
And you need a compelling
offer to the customer

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:20.700
and you need it to be
consistent and reliable,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:24.250
because if you got it
wrong, they will move.

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:27.510
Our warehouses allow always
to offer a wider range

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:30.900
than anybody else, about
50,000 grocery lines.

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:37.890
And we achieve a 99% fulfillment
rate on that to the customer.

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:41.390
But you know, it
doesn't always work.

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:44.790
And as much as we've applied
machine learning inside

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:48.030
of our warehouses to improve
the way the product flows and is

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:52.290
laid out, and as much as we're
intelligent about segmenting

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:57.090
and personalizing our service,
when we get a customer problem,

00:19:57.090 --> 00:20:02.790
it always comes down, as Apoorv
said, to emails or voice.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.190
And when you get to
a scale that we're

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:08.800
at now, dealing with those
emails can be a challenge.

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:11.820
And that's where we hope
machine learning would help us.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:13.260
SARA ROBINSON: So how are
you using natural language

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:15.330
processing and machine
learning, and specifically

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:19.710
Google Cloud Platform products,
to solve that problem?

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:23.370
DAN NELSON: So this is a
typical contact center-- i.e.

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.410
it was our contact center.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:27.300
An email would come
in from a customer.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:30.960
It would go behind the
queue, and eventually it

00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:33.360
would get to the
front to be processed.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:36.540
You can see that regardless
of the type of email,

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:39.440
regardless of the importance
and the sentiment,

00:20:39.440 --> 00:20:42.600
that queuing system just worked.

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:46.230
Now we get about 2,000 emails
a day into our contact centers,

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:49.700
and we resource our contact
centers to deal with that.

00:20:49.700 --> 00:20:54.180
Where it becomes a problem
is if we have a road network

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:55.300
problem--

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:57.630
it's the UK, we
get bad weather--

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.180
then actually that
operation can slow down

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:03.170
and the emails can
double, even triple.

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:05.100
And so we wanted to
come up with a way

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:10.720
to basically prioritize so we
could get rid of the queue.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.410
And as Apoorv had
explained, that's

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.830
kind of bringing
structure to these emails.

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:18.420
So the idea here--

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:19.270
I love your website.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.810
These are typically
emails I have to say.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:24.860
I have children so it's easier
for me to do shopping online.

00:21:24.860 --> 00:21:28.050
That is classified as feedback.

00:21:28.050 --> 00:21:30.140
It's positive.

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.200
And that's how we are able
to then take that forward.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:37.870
Now we used Natural
Language API initially.

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.370
And that did some great
work in the classification

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:42.830
and really good in sentiment.

00:21:42.830 --> 00:21:45.050
But the structure of our
emails and the language

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:47.540
our customers used
means that we need

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:50.330
a bit more of a bespoke model.

00:21:50.330 --> 00:21:52.340
And certainly the
tags we want applying

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:56.580
are very much fitting
to our business.

00:21:56.580 --> 00:22:00.410
So as Apoorv had talked
about, we thought, well,

00:22:00.410 --> 00:22:03.560
we'll look at using TensorFlow.

00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:07.580
We actually store all of our
customer emails as an archive.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:12.530
We have a corpus of about
3 million emails stored.

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:14.970
They were actually
all stored on-premise.

00:22:14.970 --> 00:22:16.850
And before we push
them into GCS,

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:19.010
we had to do a
load of data prep.

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:23.240
We wanted to remove all of the
personally identifiable data.

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:26.060
People do put credit cards,
a bit like the demo today.

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.450
People can be a little easy
with their information.

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:31.460
So we ran some preprocessing.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:34.250
And once that had been
stripped out, we tokenized it.

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:37.250
So we kind of
retained the meaning.

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:41.060
We pushed that into GCS,
and then basically ran

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:44.450
a training of a model.

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:48.320
And once that had been built,
we, as Apoorv had suggested,

00:22:48.320 --> 00:22:50.270
put it into Cloud ML.

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:53.180
And it now sits in front
of our contact center,

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:56.330
and in real time,
receives the email,

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:58.910
works out what it believes
the subject matter is,

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:02.720
what the sentiment is, what the
priority is, and then pushes

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:06.410
that back into the queue
in the correct order.

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:13.710
Basically the model is a
convolutional neural network.

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:16.650
We have a word embedding
layer followed by two

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:20.190
parallel convolutional layers.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:25.260
We have a performance
measure of 0.8.

00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:29.040
And the model was trained using
word2vec as an initialization

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:31.290
into the word embedding.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.810
We found that it worked
really well on our corpus.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:35.790
And so we retained that.

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:38.240
And we trained with a
sigmoid cross entropy lost

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.617
over 20 epochs.

00:23:40.617 --> 00:23:42.200
SARA ROBINSON: So
how has implementing

00:23:42.200 --> 00:23:43.760
all this machine
learning functionality

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:44.750
impacting your business?

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:46.220
And maybe you could
share any lessons you've

00:23:46.220 --> 00:23:47.480
learned throughout the process.

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:48.438
DAN NELSON: Yeah, sure.

00:23:48.438 --> 00:23:50.840
I was bit quick on the
click there wasn't I.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:56.410
So we put this in December,
run it in parallel basically

00:23:56.410 --> 00:24:00.680
to our normal
contact center queue.

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:04.910
And we got some stock results.

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.700
We were four times
quicker at responding

00:24:07.700 --> 00:24:13.550
to negatively sentiment flagged
emails than we were responding

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:14.554
to the positive ones.

00:24:14.554 --> 00:24:15.470
So that was brilliant.

00:24:15.470 --> 00:24:18.890
So there was a definite
distinction and differentiation

00:24:18.890 --> 00:24:19.640
there.

00:24:19.640 --> 00:24:23.030
And actually as a base level,
we were three times quicker

00:24:23.030 --> 00:24:24.950
responding to those
negative emails

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:27.110
than if we hadn't
applied filtering.

00:24:27.110 --> 00:24:29.450
So that was fantastic.

00:24:29.450 --> 00:24:33.770
Ironically, we haven't
actually had a bad day since.

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:37.220
So we haven't got a measure
for what it does when we spike.

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:40.340
But that was some
fantastic results.

00:24:40.340 --> 00:24:43.040
And then as part of
kind of better data,

00:24:43.040 --> 00:24:46.970
we actually found out that
about 10% of those emails

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.210
don't require any human
interaction at all.

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:53.310
They're kind of things like
thanks, something like that.

00:24:53.310 --> 00:24:57.590
And if we auto close
those, then we can save,

00:24:57.590 --> 00:25:00.320
redeploy four full
time employees

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:01.960
in the contact center.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:07.670
It's going to save us sort of
around about 100,000 pounds

00:25:07.670 --> 00:25:09.710
a year.

00:25:09.710 --> 00:25:14.420
And as the model itself only
costs us 1,000 pounds a year

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:18.230
to run inside of
Cloud ML engine,

00:25:18.230 --> 00:25:20.886
that's about a 10,000% ROI.

00:25:20.886 --> 00:25:22.010
SARA ROBINSON: That's huge.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:24.740
DAN NELSON: It's huge.

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.382
But those weren't
the only things

00:25:26.382 --> 00:25:27.590
that kind of come out for it.

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:30.380
I mean obviously it would have
been great if that was all.

00:25:30.380 --> 00:25:33.680
But we did have some key
lessons, as you asked.

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:37.550
Firstly, we learned that we
had to define our success

00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:39.470
criteria really well.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:42.710
This was actually the business
and the development team

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:46.160
for the contact center who
came to us with the problem.

00:25:46.160 --> 00:25:49.160
And they were using measures
like time for first response

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:51.039
and things like that.

00:25:51.039 --> 00:25:52.580
Actually what we
found is that wasn't

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:55.820
a great measure because
it ignored the sentiment.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:59.600
It kind of ignored the
long term compounding.

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:02.540
If it's my first issue, does
it stop me shopping-- no.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:05.770
If it's two or three, yeah.

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:08.330
So we had to work with them
on getting better measures

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:12.470
so we could actually tally if
what we were doing was better.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:15.350
The other thing we found
is be really careful

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:17.990
when you kind of define
your control groups

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:19.250
and run A/B tests.

00:26:19.250 --> 00:26:21.710
When you're playing about
with customer service,

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.080
you don't want to
get your test wrong.

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:27.470
And you want to identify as soon
as something might be wrong.

00:26:27.470 --> 00:26:30.470
And then the other thing
was the initial feeling

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.750
that this was all about
contact center efficiency

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:37.190
and headcount actually
was not borne out either.

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:39.980
By better data, we're
now able to spot

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.800
when products are starting
to behave abnormally

00:26:42.800 --> 00:26:44.510
from a refund level.

00:26:44.510 --> 00:26:46.580
Sometimes that's something
we do operationally

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:49.550
to do with the way we've
received or store product.

00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:51.110
If we respond to
that quicker, we

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:54.440
can reduce refunds
going to customers.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:56.410
Refunds are basically
bad products.

00:26:56.410 --> 00:26:59.510
So that's a great
customer service benefit.

00:26:59.510 --> 00:27:01.572
And we can also respond
to people going,

00:27:01.572 --> 00:27:02.530
I'm not going to be in.

00:27:02.530 --> 00:27:04.580
Please cancel.

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:06.860
And that means we can
free up resorts on vans

00:27:06.860 --> 00:27:11.780
and use less picking, pick
less product, less waste.

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:15.890
And then the better data
also we can then extend out

00:27:15.890 --> 00:27:18.030
into our lapsing model.

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:22.100
We now understand not just you
contacted the contact center,

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:23.750
but it was positive or negative.

00:27:23.750 --> 00:27:25.140
It was about a product.

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:27.860
And that helps us build better
lapsing models because now we

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:30.056
know the contact.

00:27:30.056 --> 00:27:32.180
But more than anything--
and it's a relatively soft

00:27:32.180 --> 00:27:33.080
benefit--

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:37.670
we now really understand how
to build these machine learning

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:40.320
models, how easy it is.

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.050
And with the right
kind of thought,

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:44.240
it's kind of opened up
vast opportunities for us

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:45.340
to work on this.

00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:45.830
SARA ROBINSON: Definitely.

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:46.880
So what's next for Ocado?

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:49.130
Do you have any future plans
for more machine learning

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:49.713
functionality?

00:27:49.713 --> 00:27:50.990
DAN NELSON: Oh yeah, yep.

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:53.690
So this is only the
beginning, really,

00:27:53.690 --> 00:27:55.130
the contact center piece.

00:27:55.130 --> 00:27:59.235
We'd love to apply this to
social networks as well.

00:27:59.235 --> 00:28:01.610
Contact center would love us
to be able to pick up trends

00:28:01.610 --> 00:28:03.170
from there.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.210
We want to go more
into conversation

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:08.690
as well, so more
on the speech side.

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:11.690
Could be on the retail side
or from a customer service

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:13.640
point of view.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:16.880
We're also looking now at
deploying machine learning

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:19.646
models for demand forecasting.

00:28:19.646 --> 00:28:21.290
Our forecasting is
great at the minute.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:24.800
We only have less than
1% of sales as waste.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:27.290
But we think we can get better.

00:28:27.290 --> 00:28:30.606
And so the opportunities are
vast within the operation.

00:28:30.606 --> 00:28:31.480
SARA ROBINSON: Great.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:33.187
Thank you so much for
sharing your use case.

00:28:33.187 --> 00:28:33.654
DAN NELSON: You're welcome.

00:28:33.654 --> 00:28:34.588
Thank you.

00:28:38.599 --> 00:28:39.640
SARA ROBINSON: Thank you.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:41.514
So next I'd like to
bring up Apoorv and Rajiv

00:28:41.514 --> 00:28:43.920
to talk about PwC,
another customer use case.

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:51.180
APOORV SAXENA: [INAUDIBLE] Dan.

00:28:51.180 --> 00:28:54.100
This totally changes a
perception of a grocery store,

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:55.110
doesn't it?

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:58.110
How many times do you think
machine learning is being

00:28:58.110 --> 00:29:01.800
used in such interesting ways?

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:06.150
And this is unique in a way
because not every customer

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:08.820
that we work with has
a data science team.

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:12.330
And I'm privileged to have a
head of the data science here.

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:15.870
But what if the customer
doesn't have a data science team

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:19.200
and wants to achieve the same
sort of impactful results?

00:29:19.200 --> 00:29:22.940
And this is where our
partners such as PwC come in.

00:29:22.940 --> 00:29:26.370
So to talk about how customers
are working with PwC to build

00:29:26.370 --> 00:29:28.980
an end to end solution,
I'll hand it over to Rajiv

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:29.835
to talk about it.

00:29:29.835 --> 00:29:31.084
RAJIV SALIMATH: Thanks Apoorv.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:37.660
So I'm going to talk about
one of our customers,

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:41.170
Oscar Larson, and how we helped
them with machine learning,

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:44.980
how we helped bring
optimization to their business.

00:29:44.980 --> 00:29:48.670
But to set the
expectation, I think

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:50.620
Google has a great
technology stack

00:29:50.620 --> 00:29:51.910
across all kinds of things.

00:29:51.910 --> 00:29:53.440
And I always joke
with my friends

00:29:53.440 --> 00:29:55.030
that you know
Google's stack is good

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:59.020
because it can recognize Indian
names in the voice recognition

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.840
thing, which is very hard to do.

00:30:02.840 --> 00:30:08.770
And Oscar Larson is a company
that that's a PwC client.

00:30:08.770 --> 00:30:12.640
And they service gas stations.

00:30:12.640 --> 00:30:16.300
And they run the largest
such service companies.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:19.120
And they have a huge challenge
across different parts

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:21.940
of their customer
support organization.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:23.380
They have a
challenge across, how

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:27.130
do you optimize recording
of the calls that

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:30.790
come in from different
gas stations about what

00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:32.510
might be down?

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:35.560
How do you make sure that the
field engineers who go out

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:38.110
there have all
the data that they

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.600
need, they have all the right
equipment that they need,

00:30:40.600 --> 00:30:42.670
the parts, so
they're well-equipped

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:45.010
and they don't have
to do a return trip?

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:46.900
And at the same
time, we also wanted

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:48.910
to analyze how we
solve the problem

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:51.100
that parts and
other such inventory

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:54.400
are not stuck out on the
trucks of these field engineers

00:30:54.400 --> 00:30:58.090
and they're using the right
things for the right solutions.

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:00.430
And Larson, as a
company, they wanted

00:31:00.430 --> 00:31:04.390
to get a scale from being a
dominant player in five states

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:07.060
to grow across the country.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:10.060
And what PwC was
able to do was we

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:14.530
were able to bring this
business data to kind of train

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:17.830
the machine learning
algorithms and use the Larson

00:31:17.830 --> 00:31:20.260
data, along with
the proprietary data

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:23.110
that we were able to bring,
to build them a solution that

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:26.290
could help them scale.

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:29.760
And the way we did this--

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.310
so we kind of, when
you look at the system

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:36.150
that we built for Larson,
essentially every time

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:39.750
a new call comes in
at the left side,

00:31:39.750 --> 00:31:42.450
and initially we
first try to extract

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:44.640
whatever useful
concept and entities

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:46.800
that we can extract from there.

00:31:46.800 --> 00:31:48.930
It's a little bit
like one of the slides

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:52.920
that Sara had earlier where
we use the Google Cloud NLP

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:56.460
API to extract whatever
concepts we can get.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:59.940
And we also want to
extract whatever sentiments

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:01.620
that we can extract from them.

00:32:01.620 --> 00:32:03.540
It's slightly different
from a problem

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:07.170
of trying to disambiguate
whether JK Rowling is

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:10.170
the right author or
not when you have

00:32:10.170 --> 00:32:14.910
to deal with statements
like, hey, my pump is down.

00:32:14.910 --> 00:32:17.400
It is a positive
sentiment statement.

00:32:17.400 --> 00:32:20.760
But you have to try to
analyze, why is the pump down?

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:22.740
What could the problems be?

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.510
And that's what I'm going
to show you is a demo.

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.660
So to analyze the
problem that comes in,

00:32:28.660 --> 00:32:33.590
we take all of the data that
we have access to from Larson.

00:32:33.590 --> 00:32:36.870
They get over 50,000
support calls a year.

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:39.080
And they've been doing
this for over 10 years.

00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:41.790
So there's a huge data set
that was available for us

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:43.840
to use as training data.

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:47.400
And we also look at what
parts were actually sold

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:50.310
to the customers to
fix different issues.

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:52.920
And so we know that
a problem came in,

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:55.260
a certain part was
used to fix it.

00:32:55.260 --> 00:32:57.510
And we use that as
training data to figure out

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:02.270
for future problems what kind
of parts might be relevant.

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:06.650
And using that, the ML
system that we built,

00:33:06.650 --> 00:33:09.230
it recommends three solutions.

00:33:09.230 --> 00:33:11.510
Anytime somebody
calls into Larson

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:14.090
and says there's a
problem, it figures out

00:33:14.090 --> 00:33:16.220
who's the most qualified
engineer, who's

00:33:16.220 --> 00:33:20.030
the engineer who knows how
to fix that type of fuel pump

00:33:20.030 --> 00:33:22.730
or that type of point
of sale terminal.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:27.170
It figures out, what are the
right manuals and documents

00:33:27.170 --> 00:33:31.220
that he might need on the
ground to refer quickly

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:32.760
to fix that issue?

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:36.890
And we have all of those
stored in Google Drive.

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:40.550
And the machine learning
automatically, in real time,

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:43.820
links these Google
Docs to the context.

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:47.660
And the last part, which
leads to the most cost saving

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:53.420
for Larson, is how it recommends
the most relevant parts.

00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:55.190
I'll show a quick demo of this.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:33:59.945
If I can switch over
to the other screen?

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:12.659
APOORV SAXENA: So
[INAUDIBLE] right now.

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:15.449
RAJIV SALIMATH: Yeah, well we
Indians have as many machine

00:34:15.449 --> 00:34:18.290
learning experts as we
have gas station owners.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:22.920
So for this context let's
assume Apoorv is a gas station

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:26.520
guy who really doesn't know much
about what the problem could

00:34:26.520 --> 00:34:27.960
be in his gas station.

00:34:27.960 --> 00:34:32.400
And this is what 90% of the
problems that the Larson

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:35.600
customer support team looks at.

00:34:35.600 --> 00:34:37.889
They could get a
problem as simple

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:41.850
as something like just
my pumps are down.

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:44.400
And in most cases,
these people don't

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.590
know how to describe
anything more than that.

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:49.770
They have no clue
what makes up a pump.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:52.710
I've had an education in
this in the last six months.

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.330
I've been forced to study this.

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.150
But most people don't know.

00:34:56.150 --> 00:35:00.120
So the system figures out
from a historic pattern

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:03.360
of all of the problems.

00:35:03.360 --> 00:35:09.300
And it does Cloud MLP on the
fly to first figure out, hey,

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:10.380
all the pumps are down.

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:13.500
Are they down on diesel
pumps, unleaded pumps?

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:16.260
It tries to get
some more context.

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:25.840
And if I say, for example, all
pumps are down on unleaded,

00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:28.570
and then it
understands that, hey,

00:35:28.570 --> 00:35:30.640
if it's a problem
with just one pump,

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:33.520
then it's probably something
to do with parts that

00:35:33.520 --> 00:35:35.290
make up a specific fuel pump.

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:38.770
And then it looks at things
like breakaways and hoses

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:40.080
and things like that.

00:35:40.080 --> 00:35:42.160
But if it's something to
do with a lot of pumps,

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:46.270
then it's probably something
to do with communication

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:48.660
to the pump, a motor,
something like that.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:51.550
So at first it picks up--
it also has a Google Map

00:35:51.550 --> 00:35:52.940
API integrated with it.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:54.880
So it picks up the location.

00:35:54.880 --> 00:35:57.151
It picks up where
Apoorv is calling from.

00:35:57.151 --> 00:35:58.900
He's definitely not
calling from New York,

00:35:58.900 --> 00:36:03.190
but that's a real gas
station in New York.

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:10.620
And once he confirms
his location,

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:12.810
it picks up what
type of gas pumps

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:15.100
there are in that location.

00:36:15.100 --> 00:36:20.450
And then it diagnoses
the issue to say

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:23.980
that it's probably something to
do with the motor or a filter.

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:26.540
And it now sends
this information

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:29.450
to the field engineer who's
been assigned to that case.

00:36:29.450 --> 00:36:31.820
It also picks a
field engineer who

00:36:31.820 --> 00:36:36.410
is equipped in fixing motor
or filter types of issues.

00:36:36.410 --> 00:36:39.140
And I can show you
a little bit more

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:43.110
under the covers of
what's happening.

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:45.570
So this is a simple
representation

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:49.000
of the recommendation
engine that we've built.

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:54.720
And so when that chatbot
looks at the back end,

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:56.640
it first tries to
figure out, what's

00:36:56.640 --> 00:36:59.310
the equipment on that site?

00:36:59.310 --> 00:37:03.440
And then based on
recommending the issues--

00:37:03.440 --> 00:37:05.250
let me just refresh this.

00:37:23.780 --> 00:37:27.680
So it first analyzes that
particular site and figure out

00:37:27.680 --> 00:37:30.650
what type of equipment
there is on that site.

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:33.830
And once it understands
that, it goes on

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:36.080
to try to analyze the problem.

00:37:36.080 --> 00:37:38.600
And this is we're
using TensorFlow.

00:37:38.600 --> 00:37:41.480
And we have a single
layered neural network

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:44.360
to-- we created a single
layered neural network

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:46.410
to solve that problem.

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:49.040
And once it figures
out what could

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:54.560
be the different equipment that
could be causing the fault,

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:58.790
it creates a simple Bayesian
probability analysis of that,

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:00.500
and then assesses
that it's probably

00:38:00.500 --> 00:38:04.040
due to a motor or
a filter fault.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:06.750
Then the hardest
part of this product

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:09.230
was to try to figure out
what's the right part that

00:38:09.230 --> 00:38:10.910
could solve that issue.

00:38:10.910 --> 00:38:13.010
And this is something
that we're picking out

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:17.210
of literally several
thousand parts on the fly.

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:19.070
And once it finds
the right part,

00:38:19.070 --> 00:38:20.810
it also recommends
the right documents

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:24.890
that that field engineer
would need to refer

00:38:24.890 --> 00:38:27.020
to fix that issue on site.

00:38:27.020 --> 00:38:28.820
We can come back to the slide.

00:38:35.610 --> 00:38:37.070
APOORV SAXENA: So
I think one thing

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:38.528
I'm sure the audience
want to know,

00:38:38.528 --> 00:38:40.860
what is the
infrastructure and what

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:44.315
are the technology
choices PwC made in sort

00:38:44.315 --> 00:38:45.840
of building the solutions?

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:47.427
So could you tell more about it?

00:38:47.427 --> 00:38:48.510
RAJIV SALIMATH: Yeah sure.

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:54.480
So this is what our
architecture looks like.

00:38:54.480 --> 00:39:01.710
And we chose GCP primarily
because a lot of the tools

00:39:01.710 --> 00:39:02.580
that we needed--

00:39:02.580 --> 00:39:04.870
we've tried a lot
of different tools.

00:39:04.870 --> 00:39:08.675
And for example, if you look
at the bottom of that slide

00:39:08.675 --> 00:39:12.480
where we have all of the
NLP and ML components--

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:15.540
so we used the
Natural Language API

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:19.680
and we used the Cloud Machine
Learning API, and TensorFlow.

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:23.600
And when we have a lot of these
call records, a lot of these

00:39:23.600 --> 00:39:26.660
are not entered
in full sentences.

00:39:26.660 --> 00:39:28.780
These are field engineers
who are entering stuff

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:30.350
on their phone.

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:31.730
It's broken language.

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:35.750
It's a lot of code
words and item numbers

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:38.160
and part names and
things say that.

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:43.520
So it's very hard to
process that type of data

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:48.890
with just a natural
language processing.

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:52.170
And because there's a good
tethering between the NLP

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:55.940
API, the machine learning,
and creating a neural network

00:39:55.940 --> 00:40:00.050
and executing it on TensorFlow
in the Google framework,

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:02.180
we chose that platform.

00:40:02.180 --> 00:40:06.230
And on the right side, you
can see the legacy data,

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:10.800
which is the data set that
Larson had for over 10 years.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:15.140
And we migrated that data
to essentially a Cloud SQL

00:40:15.140 --> 00:40:16.190
database.

00:40:16.190 --> 00:40:19.940
And we used Firebase on top
of it, along with BigQuery,

00:40:19.940 --> 00:40:24.260
to create indexes
that we can query.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:27.560
And the front end
is all built as--

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:30.740
we have different apps
for the dispatcher.

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:34.940
We have apps for the call center
people, field tech people.

00:40:34.940 --> 00:40:37.640
These are all Android
apps which all access

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:41.000
code through App Engine.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:44.150
The reason we chose
GCP was it really

00:40:44.150 --> 00:40:47.540
provided all the elements we
needed, from the front end

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:51.590
to the back end to the
machine learning piece.

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:52.670
APOORV SAXENA: Thank you.

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:58.300
So to wrap it up, I think,
as you can see, if you are--

00:40:58.300 --> 00:40:58.800
if you--

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:01.280
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:02.272
Thank you.

